aften 0.09pipe1 - change notes
==============================

Base: upstream aften, SourceForge git snapshot 89aee3d (Jan 2011,
"0.08 + git"), kept byte-identical.  The encoder, the bitstream, the
API and the PCM library are untouched; the handful of fixes below are
all that this fork adds.  Upstream authorship (Justin Ruggles, Prakash
Punnoor, et al.) is preserved in the file headers and in git history.

This is a personal, for-fun build for continued use of a great little
tool.  Upstream has been quiet since 2011, so this is not an attempt
to take over anything, and there is nothing to credit to anyone here.

Patches (see git log for the exact commits)
-------------------------------------------
1. pcm/wav.c  (wave_init)
   Streaming muxers (FFmpeg et al.) write 0xFFFFFFFF as placeholder
   data chunk size when the output is a pipe.  That value read into an
   int is -1, which sign-extends into a bogus data_size of
   0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF when assigned to the 64-bit field; the derived
   sample count overflowed into nonsense (broken progress display).
   On non-seekable input the placeholder is now treated as
   size-unknown: read to end-of-stream.  Streamed WAV input works
   without -readtoeof 1.

2. aften/opts.c
   stdin is auto-detected: if no input filename is given and stdin is
   not a terminal, "-" is used as input:
       ffmpeg -i in.wav -f wav - | aften out.ac3
       aften out.ac3 < in.wav
   Explicit "aften - out.ac3" works as it has since 2006.

3. aften/aften.c
   - stdin/stdout are no longer fclose()d on exit (MSVC debug CRT
     aborts on the second close at process teardown).
   - fixed memmove size when -pad 0 and the stream is shorter than
     256 samples (copied nr bytes instead of nr*channels*sizeof(FLOAT)).

4. aften/helptext.h
   Usage and longhelp document pipe I/O ("-" filenames).  Pipe input
   and output existed in aften since 2006 but were never mentioned in
   the help text anywhere, which is the reason the tool is widely
   believed to lack the feature.

5. libaften/a52.h, Changelog
   Version string: "git" -> "0.09pipe1".

6. CMakeModules/DetectCompiler.cmake, CMakeLists.txt
   Machine type detection, which blocked every native 64-bit Windows
   build.  The 2011 code required CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR to contain
   "x86" when there is no uname, and 64-bit Windows reports
   %PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE% = "AMD64", so MSVC x64 stopped with
   "Could not detect machine type" at CMakeLists.txt:152.  Now:
     - names are normalized case-insensitively (AMD64 / X86_64 / X64 /
       X86 / I[3-6]86 -> x86, PPC / POWERPC / Power Macintosh -> ppc);
     - 32-bit is told from 64-bit by the pointer size
       (CMAKE_SIZEOF_VOID_P), the only reliable signal, since an
       "AMD64"-reporting shell can be running a 32-bit compiler;
     - ARM64 and any other CPU configure as portable C instead of
       aborting the configure, because nothing outside the x86/PPC SIMD
       blocks needs a machine type;
     - the host uname is ignored when cross-compiling, and an
       MSYS/WSL uname (which names the OS, "MSYS_NT-10.0-...") falls
       back to the processor CMake reports;
     - EXEC_PROGRAM, deprecated since CMake 3.27, became
       EXECUTE_PROCESS.
   Build system only; the encoder is untouched.

Verification
------------
Pipe encodes (stdin, ffmpeg streaming pipe, stdout, 24-bit) are
byte-identical to the file-based encodes of the same audio, and to the
upstream encoder's output (single-threaded CBR; the encoder core is
unmodified, this just proves the I/O paths are lossless).

Windows (Windows 11 x64, MSVC 19.51 from VS 2026 BuildTools, CMake
4.4.4):
  - x64 and Win32 both configure -- "Machine type: x86_64" / "i386" --
    and build.  MMX is correctly off in x64 (MSVC has no __m64 there)
    and on in Win32, where the Intel-syntax inline-asm CPU detection is
    the code path that runs.
  - file, pipe (cmd "type" | aften -), 24-bit input, -threads 1..16,
    -nosimd, /MD and /MT, x64 and i386: every encode of one 5 s stereo
    input produced the same 240384 bytes with the same MD5.
  - MSVC and gcc encodes of the same audio differ in 3 of 240384
    bytes, all of them in frames 364-365.  The DC/bandwidth/LFE filter
    coefficients are computed at runtime with cos/sqrt from libm, and
    the MSVC CRT and glibc round in the last ULP; decoded with ffmpeg
    the streams are within 2 LSB of 16-bit, on 0.03% of the samples.
    Byte-identity claims are therefore per-compiler, not across
    toolchains.

Linux (x86_64, gcc, CMake 4.4.4): unchanged -- "Machine type: x86_64",
MMX/SSE/SSE2/SSE3 detected, output identical to the released
0.09pipe1 binary.
